sdrc.desc:
CRPS, formerly known as reflex sympathetic dystrophy (RSD), is persistent pain that typically occurs after a trauma, surgery, or immobilization.

This condition may be accompanied by:
- significant hypersensitivity;
- swelling of the affected limb;
- mobility issues;
- changes in skin temperature or color.

In some situations, advanced neuromodulation techniques, such as dorsal root ganglion stimulation, may be considered following a specialized evaluation.

douleurs_articulaires.desc:
Joint pain can be related to:
- osteoarthritis;
- tendinitis;
- traumatic injuries;
- consequences of surgical interventions.

Depending on the origin of the pain, different approaches are possible, including ultrasound-guided or fluoroscopy-guided joint injections, as well as other specialized treatments aimed at restoring joint function and reducing inflammation.

douleurs_neuropathiques.desc:
Neuropathic pain results from damage or dysfunction of the nervous system.

It can manifest as:
- burning sensations;
- electric shocks;
- tingling;
- numbness;
- hypersensitivity to touch.

These pains can be linked to diabetes, surgery, trauma, shingles, or certain neurological conditions. When conventional treatments are insufficient, specialized techniques (rTMS, neuromodulation) can be proposed.

t01.desc:
Spinal and epidural injections involve injecting an anti-inflammatory or anesthetic medication near the structures responsible for the pain.

These techniques are particularly used in:
- lower back pain;
- sciatica;
- herniated discs;
- certain cervical pains.

Performed under radiological or ultrasound guidance, they provide precise and safe treatment.
